  "title": "What's a Fragment?",
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Course: Build your First Web App with React"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 1,
      "content": "What's a Fragment?"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Right now, your components wrap everything inside a <div>. That works just fine, but sometimes you don't want an extra <div> in your HTML."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "React lets you use a Fragment, which is an invisible wrapper. It groups elements without adding a DOM node."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Instead of writing:"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "react",
      "content": "return (\n  <div>\n    <input />\n    <textarea />\n    <button />\n  </div>\n);"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "You can write:"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "react",
      "content": "return (\n  <>\n    <input />\n    <textarea />\n    <button />\n  </>\n);"
    },
    {
      "type": "image",
      "alt": "Idea Emoji",
      "src": "data:image/png;base64,"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "The <> and </> are shorthand for <React.Fragment> and </React.Fragment>. They group the elements, but they don't render a <div> in the HTML."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "content": "How does it work?"
    },
